f higher learning, located in the historic town of Tübingen, Baden-Württemberg. It was founded under the auspices of Count Eberhard V of Württemberg, reflecting the early modern era's emphasis on humanism and academic inquiry. The university's significance is underscored by its role in the development of Protestant theology, particularly through its association with key figures like Johannes Kepler, who revolutionized astronomy with his laws of planetary motion, and later with the influential philosopher Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel.

